WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.163
Hi, I'm Quenby!

00:00:02.163 --> 00:00:06.237
The cheapest way to stop attention growing is to stop it looking far back.

00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.642
A normal layer lets each query see every earlier key.

00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:13.634
A growing triangle.

00:00:14.054 --> 00:00:17.350
A local layer keeps only a band, the last few keys.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.148
Every query reads the same amount.

00:00:19.568 --> 00:00:22.675
Many models also keep the first token visible to everyone.

00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:23.918
More on that soon.

00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:27.314
One window layer sees a window back.

00:00:28.075 --> 00:00:31.901
Stack another, and each position already summarises the window before it.

00:00:32.301 --> 00:00:36.431
Mistral's paper puts it at about one hundred and thirty-one thousand tokens,

00:00:36.431 --> 00:00:39.184
with a window of four thousand and ninety-six.

00:00:39.184 --> 00:00:39.872
In theory.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.492
Longformer drew the band in twenty twenty.

00:00:43.872 --> 00:00:46.006
And added a few global positions.

00:00:46.006 --> 00:00:48.495
Today's models add whole global layers instead.

00:00:49.892 --> 00:00:53.614
Window attention fails when the text length surpasses the cache size.

00:00:55.275 --> 00:00:58.827
A softmax has to put its weight somewhere, even when nothing matters.

00:00:59.247 --> 00:01:02.907
Trained models park it on the first tokens, which every query can see.

00:01:03.327 --> 00:01:06.187
Slide them out of the window, and that weight lands anywhere.

00:01:06.607 --> 00:01:09.225
So keep the first four tokens forever.

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:11.844
StreamingLLM ran four million tokens that way.

00:01:13.583 --> 00:01:14.714
Modern models interleave.

00:01:14.714 --> 00:01:19.240
MiMo stacks five window layers of just a hundred and twenty-eight tokens

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.502
under one global layer, eight times.

00:01:21.922 --> 00:01:24.471
The global layers carry every long-range lookup.

00:01:24.471 --> 00:01:27.747
The windows carry everything local, at a fixed cost.

00:01:28.925 --> 00:01:31.163
Gemma 4 does it one in six.

00:01:31.163 --> 00:01:33.402
Fifty window layers hold under a gigabyte.

00:01:33.402 --> 00:01:36.919
Made full, the same model would need two hundred and twenty-five.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.111
A window caps memory by position.

00:01:40.111 --> 00:01:43.127
A global layer every few reads the whole past.

00:01:45.108 --> 00:01:47.961
A band, a sink, and a global layer every few.

00:01:47.961 --> 00:01:48.817
Thanks for watching!

00:01:49.708 --> 00:01:51.810
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:52.410
I'm Quenby.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:52.710
Bye!
